#!/usr/bin/env bash
# pre-push -- refuse a push whose working tree does not match HEAD.
#
# A push carries commits. Three PRs in one day were pushed with a file the
# author had edited but never committed, and each looked fine locally for
# exactly that reason: #75 added Go sources and left them out of
# gazelle/BUILD.bazel, #74 did the same to a _test.go file and turned main red
# for hours, and #85 pushed a rename while the fix and three assertions it
# needed stayed in the worktree.
#
# Untracked files count. That is the shape of #74 and #75 -- a new source file
# that was never `git add`ed -- and .gitignore already covers the untracked
# things a worktree really does carry (bazel-* symlinks, node_modules, .bazel/,
# target/, .claude/).
#
# Advisory only: it runs from `git push` in a checkout that has been pointed at
# this directory (`git config core.hooksPath .githooks`, see CONTRIBUTING.md),
# and `git push --no-verify` skips it.

set -euo pipefail

dirty="$(git status --porcelain --untracked-files=normal)"
[ -n "$dirty" ] || exit 0

cat >&2 <<MESSAGE
pre-push: the working tree does not match HEAD.

$(printf '%s\n' "$dirty" | sed 's/^/  /')

A push carries commits, not the working tree, so nothing listed above is in
what you are about to push -- CI, and every reviewer, sees the change without
it. Commit or stash it first:

  git add -A && git commit          commit it
  git stash --include-untracked     set it aside

To push anyway: git push --no-verify
MESSAGE
exit 1
